    - analysis_quality  # 96fa
    isbefore: []  # 96fa
    duplicate_of: []  # 96fa
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 96fa
    pr_ref:  # 96fa
    description: "The drift linter find_axis_drift (in hypergumbo_core.edge_types, used by both the in-tree property test in test_edge_types.py and the pre-commit CLI scripts/check-edge-type-drift) hard-codes the substring 'EDGE_TYPE' for matching consumer-side sets. This restricts drift detection to the one field (Edge.edge_type) currently covered by the canonical registry. As ADR-0024's axis-declaration template gets instantiated for other fields (Symbol.kind, evidence_type, supply_chain.tier, etc.), each field will need its own analogous drift detector — and copy-pasting find_axis_drift per field would produce N near-identical implementations that drift from each other.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md) and PR #3459 established the canonical edge-type registry plus the static drift detector. PR #3462 hoisted the detector into find_axis_drift so it is shared between the property test and the pre-commit linter. ADR-0024 (PR #3468) extracts the axis-declaration template — every multi-value field's axis follows the same four-part shape (registry / linter / view / property test) and the seven-step declaration workflow.\n\nThe current find_axis_drift, however, is single-field:\n- It searches for module-level *EDGE_TYPE*-named assignments.\n- It checks elements against EDGE_TYPES (the Edge.edge_type registry).\n- It returns offender strings formatted for that one field.\n\nADR-0024 §'Declaration workflow' step 3 says 'land a drift-coherence linter at scripts/check-<field>-drift'. If each field gets its own linter as a copy-paste of the existing one, we will have N near-identical linters drifting from each other. Better to share infrastructure: parameterize the existing helper.\n\nDELIVERABLES:\n\n1. PARAMETERIZE find_axis_drift across (registry_names, name_filter):\n   - Signature change shape: a field-agnostic core helper accepting (repo_root, name_filter: str, registry_names: frozenset[str]) -> list[str], where name_filter is the substring matched on assignment target names ('EDGE_TYPE' for the current case; 'SYMBOL_KIND' / 'EVIDENCE_TYPE' / etc. for future cases).\n   - The current Edge-specific public API (find_axis_drift in hypergumbo_core.edge_types with default Edge parameters) is preserved as a thin wrapper.\n\n2. RELOCATE the field-agnostic helper:\n   - Either keep find_axis_drift in hypergumbo_core.edge_types and have other registry modules import it.\n   - Or move it to a shared module (e.g., hypergumbo_core.axis_drift) that field-specific modules import.\n   The second is cleaner for ADR-0024 — a new axis declaration imports the shared function rather than recreating it. Matches ADR-0024's intent that future axes inherit infrastructure.\n\n3. UPDATE the calling sites:\n   - test_edge_types.py — pass the Edge-specific parameters (or use the back-compat wrapper).\n   - scripts/check-edge-type-drift — same.\n   - Future <field>_types.py modules import the parameterized helper and provide their own (registry_names, name_filter).\n\n4. TEST COVERAGE:\n   - Unit tests for the parameterized helper using synthetic registries (e.g., a tiny fake registry of 3 names plus a fake name_filter).\n   - Confirm existing Edge-specific tests still pass.\n   - Document the parameterization shape in ADR-0024 §Enforcement so future axis declarations inherit it.\n\n5. BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY:\n   - The public hypergumbo_core.edge_types.find_axis_drift signature (single argument: repo_root) should not break. Either keep it as a thin wrapper that calls the parameterized core with Edge-specific defaults, or document the signature change in CHANGELOG and update all known callers in the same PR.\n\nCONSIDERATIONS:\n- This generalization should be designed JOINTLY with the find_axis_drift scope-broadening (sibling: WI-zisit-hagud-rifib-sozok-pofid-javot-pihoz-lumar) since both touch the helper's signature. Landing both at once avoids two refactor passes — the cleanest shape supports both per-field name patterns AND multi-root scanning in one parameterization.\n- The name_filter substring shape may be too loose for some fields. Future Symbol.kind sets might be named *KIND*, which matches lots of unrelated things (e.g., 'KEYWORD_KIND' in a parser, 'NUMERIC_KIND' in a token type table). The filter shape may need refinement (e.g., regex, exact suffix, multi-substring intersection). Worth a small experiment during implementation — start with substring, tighten if false positives appear.\n- The exclusion of /tests/ paths (currently hardcoded) should remain — fixture data legitimately uses arbitrary string sets in tests. Other path exclusions (migrations/, vendored deps) might need to become parameters too if specific axes have different exclusion needs.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (all SOFT):\n- ADR-0024 (PR #3468) is already merged — this generalization is the implementation companion to ADR-0024's template.\n- WI-zisit-hagud-rifib-sozok-pofid-javot-pihoz-lumar (find_axis_drift scope-broadening) — co-design jointly; recommend ONE PR for both generalizations.\n- Specific axis declarations for Symbol.kind, evidence_type, etc. (not yet filed as items) need NOT land first — this generalization is preparatory infrastructure. Each future axis declaration becomes cheaper after this generalization lands.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- find_axis_drift (or an underlying parameterized helper) accepts field-agnostic (registry_names, name_filter) parameters.\n- The Edge.edge_type case still works via the existing API (no breakage in test_edge_types.py or scripts/check-edge-type-drift).\n- ADR-0024 §Enforcement section gets a one-paragraph update naming the shared drift function and how new axis declarations call it.\n- A synthetic test fixture exercises the parameterized helper with a non-edge-type registry (e.g., a fake 3-value registry plus name_filter 'FAKE_KIND').\n\nSIBLINGS:\n- WI-zisit-hagud-rifib-sozok-pofid-javot-pihoz-lumar — find_axis_drift scope-broadening. Co-design jointly.\n- WI-variv-lujug-fobig-nojit-piraz-lojam-lizil-dosip — drift-linter-tightening (gated on Phase 4b). Parameterization lands first; the tightening builds on it.\n- ADR-0024 (PR #3468) — the axis-declaration template that depends on this infrastructure being field-agnostic.\n- Future adjacent-concept-sweep tracker items for Symbol.kind, evidence_type, etc. — first downstream consumers of the parameterized helper.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as loose-end #18 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). The hardcoded substring was deliberate at first ship in PR #3459 / PR #3462 — only one field had a registry, so generalization was premature. ADR-0024 (PR #3468) made the multi-field future explicit; this item makes the infrastructure match."  # 96fa
    fields: {}  # 96fa
